aborting with a message


Let's get that out of the way first: do you know how to make it work reliably on all platforms, from your laptop to a 16-bit micro-controller? I don't. I don't even have the abort part down. Asserts get us very close, but I believe they're not guaranteed to trigger depending on compilation options.


That one could be made safer even by explicitly aborting instead of overflowing on stack. It's already going to be either incorrect or crash, so [...] returning an error [...] would be an improvement.


Actually it would make things worse. Much, much worse. We tend to see stack overflow as this big scary boogeyman, but compared to what you're proposing it is actually relatively harmless. Returning early with an error code is portable, easy... It's a trap! Consider:

u8 *hash;          size_t hash_size;
const u8 *key;     size_t key_size;
const u8 *message; size_t message_size;

// TODO: initialize the above here

crypto_blake2b_ctx ctx;
crypto_blake2b_keyed_init(&ctx, hash_size, key, key_size);
crypto_blake2b_update    (&ctx, message, message_size);
crypto_blake2b_final     (&ctx, hash);


The point here is calling init(), update(), and final() in sequence. Now let our key be too big. Stack overflow, UB, nasal demons, remote code execution, the works. That's bad, so let's return early with an error code. First problem, the user must now write:

crypto_blake2b_ctx ctx;
if (crypto_blake2b_keyed_init(&ctx, hash_size, key, key_size)) {
    panic("oops");
}
crypto_blake2b_update    (&ctx, message, message_size);
crypto_blake2b_final     (&ctx, hash);


But what if the user "knows" their inputs are correct? They don't need the check. In fact they may be unable to trigger the panic even in their unit tests. Many would thus omit the check. And if they're wrong, here's what they actually get:

// uninitialised struct
crypto_blake2b_ctx ctx;

// Wrong input, return doing nothing
crypto_blake2b_keyed_init(&ctx, hash_size, key, key_size);

// ctx still uninitialised
// We now get an uninitialised read.
crypto_blake2b_update(&ctx, message, message_size);

// Forget it, your program is toast at this point.
crypto_blake2b_final(&ctx, hash);


About as bad as the stack overflow, and arguably harder to detect. But okay, let's apply the same early return with an error code to update() and final() as well: they can look at ctx, and return with an error if it's invalid. Well this is the worst, most unsafe option by far: first, you still have the uninitialised read. Second, the final hash won't be initialised, and may now contain secrets that lived in that memory region (heartbleed I believe?), or a known constant (typically all zeroes) if hash was statically allocated. The known constant is especially pernicious, because tests are less likely to catch it. It appears to work, when in fact you enable trivial forgeries.

You could try non-portable attributes to nag the user with warnings about unchecked returns. I don't do non-portable, but let's see what it would do to user code:

crypto_blake2b_ctx ctx;
if (crypto_blake2b_keyed_init(&ctx, hash_size, key, key_size)) {
    panic("oops");
}
if (crypto_blake2b_update(&ctx, message, message_size)) {
    panic("oops again");
}
if (crypto_blake2b_final(&ctx, hash)) {
    panic("oops again, dammit");
}


The logic drowns under the error checking. We can recover some readability with a CHECK() macro (I often do that when making several system calls in a row), but that's still not ideal: users who can write correct programs are being punished by others who aren't as rigorous, and frankly should perhaps not write C in the first place. Thankfully my API is easy to bind to safer languages, you can insert all the checks there (in fact I'd strongly encourage binding authors to do so).


I know the x25519 input is not correct either and I expect crypto primitives not to care. But I really expect an "easy to use" library to explicitly fail in those cases and protect me.


If you read the front page you will note that "easy to use" is not in the contract. The promises are a little different. As for protecting you, I mostly can't: Monocypher is in the NaCl family (NaCl, TweetNaCl, libsodium), which makes it a low level library. With the exception of AEAD it's only a collection of primitives, I can't stop you from holding them wrong. For that kind of safety you need an API to a high level protocol.

